A cached icon is only as good as the rules that drew it

0017 changed how every icon on the home screen is drawn: no white adaptive
tile around an icon that is not adaptive, no baked shadow under it. On a clean
install that is all there is to it, because there is no cache yet. An update is
the other half, and every update keeps the data image -- books, accounts,
settings, and /data/data/com.android.launcher3/databases/app_icons.db with it.

IconCache does not redraw what it already has. updateDBIcons() asks the package
manager for each application's versionCode and lastUpdateTime, compares them
with the row, and leaves the row alone when they match; IconProvider's system
state, the other thing it compares, is the locale and the SDK level. None of
that changes when the rules for drawing change, so after an update the reader
would see the new treatment on the applications they happened to reinstall and
the old white tiles and shadows on all the rest -- the one thing 0017 set out
to end, mixed icons, arrived by a different door.

The mechanism for this is already in the file: the cache's version is the key
of the database, and upstream counts LAUNCHER3_DISABLE_ICON_NORMALIZATION into
it precisely because that flag changes how an icon is drawn. The two flags 0017
turns off are of exactly that kind, so they are counted the same way, each with
its own bit so that the version says which combination drew the rows. The cost
is one re-read of every icon and label on the first start after an update, the
same cost upstream pays when that flag moves.

diff --git a/src/com/android/launcher3/IconCache.java b/src/com/android/launcher3/IconCache.java
--- a/src/com/android/launcher3/IconCache.java
+++ b/src/com/android/launcher3/IconCache.java
@@ -762,8 +762,17 @@
     private static final class IconDB extends SQLiteCacheHelper {
         private final static int DB_VERSION = 17;

+        /* PW3: the version is what tells a cached icon from a stale one, and
+         * upstream already counts one flag into it because that flag changes
+         * how an icon is drawn. 0017 turns off two more of exactly that kind,
+         * so they are counted the same way. Without this, an update that keeps
+         * the data image -- which every update does -- would go on showing the
+         * white tiles and shadows cached by the version before it, for every
+         * application whose own version and install time had not changed. */
         private final static int RELEASE_VERSION = DB_VERSION +
-                (FeatureFlags.LAUNCHER3_DISABLE_ICON_NORMALIZATION ? 0 : 1);
+                (FeatureFlags.LAUNCHER3_DISABLE_ICON_NORMALIZATION ? 0 : 1) +
+                (FeatureFlags.LEGACY_ICON_TREATMENT ? 0 : 2) +
+                (FeatureFlags.ADAPTIVE_ICON_SHADOW ? 0 : 4);

         private final static String TABLE_NAME = "icons";
         private final static String COLUMN_ROWID = "rowid";
